\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{lie}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The differential of the actual Spin action is the quadratic lift}

\p{Let #{V} be a positive-dimensional finite-dimensional real vector
space, and let #{Q} be nondegenerate. Give #{\Cl(Q)} its ordinary
finite-dimensional topology. Inside #{\Cl(Q)^\times}, evenness, reverse
norm one, and normalization of the embedded vector subspace are closed
conditions and define a subgroup. This subgroup is the actual
vector-generated #{\Spin(Q)}. Indeed, for any element #{x} satisfying
them, its twisted action lies in #{O(Q)}. Cartan--Dieudonne supplies a
vector product #{u} with the same twisted action. For #{z=u^{-1}x},

##{\alpha(z)\iota(v)=\iota(v)z\qquad(v\in V).}

Graded centrality makes #{z} a nonzero scalar; no parity of #{u} has been
assumed in this step. If #{Q(w)\ne0}, every nonzero scalar #{c} is the
two-vector product

##{\iota(w)\,\iota\left(\frac{c}{Q(w)}w\right)=c1.}

Thus #{x} is vector-generated, and its stated evenness and norm place it
in Spin. The reverse inclusion is immediate from the carrier definition.
The closed-subgroup theorem now gives this concrete Spin group its
finite-dimensional real Lie-group structure.}

\p{For #{A\in\mathfrak{so}(V,B_Q)}, let #{b(A)} be the normalized
quadratic Clifford lift of \ref{fcap-000R}. For every #{t\in\mathbb R},

##{g_A(t)=\exp\bigl(t b(A)\bigr)\in\Spin(Q),
\qquad
g_A(t)\,\iota(v)\,g_A(t)^{-1}
=\iota\bigl(\exp(tA)v\bigr).}

Indeed, reversal sends #{b(A)} to #{-b(A)}, so the exponential is even
and has reverse norm one. The commutator identity
#{[b(A),\iota(v)]=\iota(Av)} gives the displayed conjugation formula by
the exponential series. Hence #{g_A(t)} satisfies the three closed
conditions above and belongs to the actual Spin group.}

\p{The tangent Lie algebra at #{1} is exactly the image of #{b}. One
inclusion is supplied by the curves #{g_A}. Conversely, let #{X} be
tangent at #{1}. Differentiating evenness, reverse norm one, and vector
normalization gives

##{X\text{ even},\qquad \operatorname{rev}(X)=-X,
\qquad [X,\iota(V)]\subseteq\iota(V).}

Write #{[X,\iota(v)]=\iota(Av)}. Differentiating preservation of #{Q}
makes #{A} skew-adjoint. Then #{X-b(A)} is even and commutes with every
vector, so it is scalar; reverse antisymmetry forces that real scalar to
vanish. Therefore

##{T_1\Spin(Q)=b\bigl(\mathfrak{so}(V,B_Q)\bigr),
\qquad d_1(\operatorname{spinVectorAction})\bigl(b(A)\bigr)=A.}

This is the genuine differential statement whose algebraic commutator
precursor appears in \ref{fcap-000R} and whose polar-dual coordinate
formula is \ref{fcap-001Z}.}

\p{If #{\pi:\Cl(Q)\to\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb R}(S)} is a
finite-dimensional real Clifford module, continuity of the power series
gives

##{\pi(g_A(t))=\exp\bigl(t\pi(b(A))\bigr),
\qquad d\pi_1\bigl(b(A)\bigr)=\pi(b(A)).}

A complex Clifford module may be regarded here as a real module; this does
not assert a separately constructed complex Lie-group structure.}

\p{\citet{Proposition 2.12, p. 40; Proposition 3.2, pp. 53--54}{meinrenken2013clifford}
proves the normalized bivector realization and the Spin
exponential/action identity.
\citet{I.2, Remark 2.1 and (2.6)--(2.7), pp. 13--14}{lawson2016spin}
independently give the ambient Clifford exponential and its conjugation
derivative.
The carrier completion and tangent converse above identify the exact
vector-generated group used here; Lawson's ambient exponential formula
alone does not supply that membership.}

\p{For the convention match, Meinrenken's bilinear form #{B_M} satisfies
#{B_Q=2B_M}. If #{(e_M^i)} is #{B_M}-dual to #{(e_i)}, then the
#{B_Q}-dual vectors are #{e_M^i/2}, and

##{b(A)=\frac12\sum_i\beta_Q\left(Ae_i,\frac12e_M^i\right)
=\frac14\sum_i\beta_Q(Ae_i,e_M^i)
=\frac14\sum_i(Ae_i)e_M^i.}

The last equality uses #{\operatorname{tr}(A)=0}; it does not rescale the
half-commutator #{\beta_Q}. TauCeti proves the algebraic lift and
generator action at the pinned revision through
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.quadraticLift_lie_ι} and the coordinate
formula \leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.soEquivQuadratic_eq_sum_bivector}.
The inspected declarations do not package the Lie-group structure,
exponential membership, or tangent converse proved above.}
